TOP(1)                         P32W Unix                          TOP(1)

NAME
       top - show the busiest processes, continually

SYNOPSIS
       top [-b] [-d seconds] [-n iterations]

DESCRIPTION
       top clears the screen of a VT100-style terminal and draws a
       summary and a table of processes, busiest first, then redraws
       it every few seconds (3 by default) until you quit. The
       screen's height is the terminal's (ttysize(3): what the
       system knows, as in a window of wm(1), where it may change
       while top runs; else $LINES), if greater than 8; else 24.

       The summary lines are:

       top - hh:mm:ss up h:mm, n users,  load average: 1, 5, 15 min
       Tasks: total, running, sleeping, zombie
       Mem: KiB total, used, free (physical memory)
       Paging: TLB refills, copy-on-write page copies, stack pages
               added, per second since the last screen (on the
               first screen: the totals since boot)

       The table has a line per process:

       PID     the process id
       USER    the owner's name (its uid if it has none)
       S       the state: R runnable or running, S sleeping, Z
               zombie, T stopped, E being created
       SIZE    its memory in bytes: up to its break, plus its
               stack
       %CPU    its share of the clock ticks since the last screen;
               on the first screen, since boot
       TIME    the CPU time used, minutes:seconds
       COMMAND the program's name

       Processes are sorted by %CPU, then by total CPU time, then by
       pid. As many are shown as fit on the screen.

OPTIONS
       -b     batch: print every process, screen after screen with
              a blank line between, without escape sequences and
              without reading keys; for logs and scripts. Use it
              with -n, or it runs until killed.
       -d seconds
              seconds between screens (at least 1).
       -n iterations
              stop after that many screens.

KEYS
       The terminal is put in cbreak mode, so keys act at once.

       q      quit (so do ^C and SIGTERM; the terminal is restored).
       k      kill: asks PID to kill: and then signal [15]:; an
              empty answer to the signal sends SIGTERM, an empty pid
              cancels.
       n      how many processes to show (0: all that fit).
       s      the seconds between screens.

       Any other key, space for instance, redraws at once. The
       questions are asked on the bottom line; backspace corrects,
       return ends the answer.

EXAMPLES
       top                    watch, until q
       top -d 1               a screen a second
       top -b -n 2 -d 5 > log two screens, five seconds apart

ENVIRONMENT
       LINES          the terminal's height, where the system
                      does not know it (default 24)
       TZ             the time zone for the clock (see tz(5))

FILES
       /etc/utmp      for the number of users
       /etc/passwd    for the user names

EXIT STATUS
       0; 2 for a bad option (with a usage line).

SEE ALSO
       ps(1), kill(1), uptime(1), time(1), sysinfo(2)

BUGS
       Only the first 64 process slots are read (the kernel has 64).
       The up time in the summary shows hours past 24 rather than
       days. Embryo and stopped processes are counted as sleeping.
       There is no sorting by other columns, no per-user filter and
       no -p pid. A question waits at most 60 seconds for each key;
       then what was typed so far is taken as the answer.
